  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">JVE Jeunes Volontaires pour l&#x2019;Environnem</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Actions to address climate change at the various levels especially at policy level have been insufficient. Africa remains most vulnerable despite being the least responsible and paying heavily for the climate change impacts while still being heavily exploited forits natural resources.
The Environmental and Climate justice Caravan is an avenue through which communities and marginalized groupscan demand for climate justice and call on decision makers at all levels to action towards effective mobilization of resources and increased commitments to fund adaptation, mitigation, loss and damage for African countries.
In the run-up to the 29th Conference ofthe Parties (COP 29) on Climate Change, climate caravans called &#x201c;Tambour Battant&#x201d; are being organised in several African countries mobilizing communities, involving movements to defend the rights of indigenous peoples, women, young people and grassroots groups. This will also target other key policy spaces, meetings and conferences beyond COP 29 with the objective of amplifying community voices, holding leaders accountable and advocating for climate action and funding to support communities and provide sustainable solutions.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">JVE is a non-governmental organisation, created in 2001, seated in Lom&#xe9;, Togo. The organisation has over 40 local branches, and through affiliates in a total of 21 countries in Africa, JVE is today known as the biggest youth-oriented environmental movement on thecontinent. Starting in a small village, working with access to information and education concerning the degrading local environment, the organisation has expanded its geographical and thematic areas of work, but still in the domain of sustainable development. Itsactivities are focused around access to energy, water and sanitation services; cultural biodiversity and eco-conscience; climate change; and natural resource management.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Jeunes Volontaires pour l&#x2019;Environne</narrative>
